WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:13.279
Hi, I'm Alex Pasek,
and I clean drains.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.749
Today we're going to 
look at a few drains,

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:18.051
and I'm going to show 
you how to clean them.

00:00:18.051 --> 00:00:19.853
When a drain is clogged,

00:00:19.853 --> 00:00:23.556
the obvious problem
is that it backs up

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:26.092
and water doesn't drain,

00:00:26.092 --> 00:00:27.360
which can cause flooding,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.329
which can lead to the drain

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:33.133
backing up further
down its run,

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:36.036
where it's even harder
to get a clog out of.

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:39.406
So regular maintenance of 
drains is really important

00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:41.307
to avoid those 
types of things.

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:47.714
Here we've got
a basin drain.

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:50.583
The water runs through
the grate on the top,

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:52.485
falls down into this basin,

00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:54.587
and the water that falls in

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:59.225
brings with it any 
of the dirt or sand

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.494
or whatever else it
picks up with it

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:02.929
into that basin.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:04.664
So the first step I do

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:06.933
is I pop off the grate top.

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:08.968
I see that it's
very backed up

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.403
with the soil and sand,

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:11.938
so I start digging,

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:15.909
just trying to expose as 
much of the drain as I can.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:18.311
I see that there's
the exit pipe,

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:22.282
and around it is a lot of 
roots mixed in with the sand.

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:24.784
So I start to kind of
break away at those,

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:26.486
eventually getting down

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:29.155
and pulling out the 
roots from the pipe.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:31.124
I get further down
into the basin,

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:32.625
and finally hit the bottom,

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:34.461
where the roots
are all coiled,

00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:36.663
and I'm able to pull
out those roots

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:38.765
all in a few big pieces.

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:41.234
And then I give the drain

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:43.403
a quick scrape-out
and clean,

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:44.871
and then pop the
lid back on,

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:46.940
and that's good to go.

00:01:53.813 --> 00:01:55.548
So, French drains
are typically

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:57.951
at the bottom of a
sloped driveway.

00:01:57.951 --> 00:01:59.152
And the reason for that is

00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:01.120
if the driveway
is on a slope

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.723
and the house is at 
the end of the slope,

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:07.794
water will continue on the 
slope and end up in the house.

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:09.662
So the French drains
are put there

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:13.933
as a way to divert the 
water away from the house.

00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:16.402
First, I'm just going
to take off the top.

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:17.437
I already can see that

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:19.772
there's quite a lot
of debris in here.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:21.508
So the first thing
I'm going to do

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:24.477
is just get my shovel and
start digging it out.

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:27.914
There's all types of different
creatures living in here.

00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:29.816
Some worms, some bugs.

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.885
The debris is made
up mostly of soil

00:02:31.885 --> 00:02:34.254
and sand and pine needles,

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:36.122
so everything 
that's good for

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.957
blocking a drain
is in there.

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:47.066
Then I'm going to start
cleaning the pipe

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:49.235
that exits this drain.

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:51.437
And obviously this 
is important because

00:02:51.471 --> 00:02:53.540
no matter how clean
the drain is,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:57.310
we're relying on that pipe
to bring out the water.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:59.345
And I don't stop
doing that until

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:00.914
either I can sort
of see light

00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:02.916
through the end of that pipe

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:05.385
or until I'm pretty certain,

00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:06.920
by shoving 
something in there,

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:08.454
that it is now cleaned out.

00:03:08.454 --> 00:03:12.091
And then I get out 
the pressure washer,

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:16.162
give the whole chamber and
the top of it a clean,

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:18.498
which just allows
for better flow

00:03:18.498 --> 00:03:21.200
and a nicer 
appearance as well.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.103
After that, I pop 
the grates back on,

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.639
and we're looking at 
a clean French drain.

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:29.976
It's important
to keep an eye

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:34.113
on the level of debris
in French drains.

00:03:34.113 --> 00:03:36.182
They can fill up
quite quickly,

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:37.784
even in the drier months.

00:03:37.784 --> 00:03:40.386
It's easy just to 
look in and see, OK,

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:42.055
there's a little bit
of stuff in here.

00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:45.692
I can no longer see the 
concrete base of the drain.

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:47.126
It's probably time
to open it up

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:49.095
and just see how
deep this is

00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:51.497
and then give it a
quick sweep out.

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:54.133
These chambers are 
fairly simple to clean,

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:56.502
and it's quite easy 
just to get a shovel

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:59.239
and start scooping
that debris out.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:02.275
This drain didn't have any
roots growing into it,

00:04:02.275 --> 00:04:03.543
which is a good sign.

00:04:03.543 --> 00:04:05.878
That means that this debris

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:08.314
hasn't probably been
there too, too long.

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:11.184
It's probably only been 
there a couple years, maybe.

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:14.053
Once all of that muck is
scooped out of there,

00:04:14.053 --> 00:04:15.855
I took the opportunity,

00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:19.359
while it was all open
and free of debris,

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:21.194
to actually give
it a good clean.

00:04:21.194 --> 00:04:24.230
So I got a pressure
washer there

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.332
and was able just to

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:29.168
clean off the sides
of the drain.

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:32.205
And that just allows
for better flow

00:04:32.205 --> 00:04:33.072
through the whole drain,

00:04:33.072 --> 00:04:35.842
as the water often 
comes in at the side,

00:04:35.842 --> 00:04:37.677
not actually right
through the top,

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:40.546
so the smoother the surface
for the water to go in

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:44.283
just allows a better 
all-over flow for the drain.

00:04:45.184 --> 00:04:47.920
I would say, you know,

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:51.057
don't be afraid to
open your drains

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:52.759
and take a look in there.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:54.060
It's not so scary.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.263
It's probably nothing 
you can break in there.

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:58.965
Really, they are
quite simple.

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:01.934
Generally just chambers
that fill up with soil.

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:05.371
No harm in opening them
up and starting to dig.

